write("predictions",
 "His ten predictions for 2026, published in the 2025 year-in-review — “the tradition of me making ill-thought-out predictions”. He scores his 2025 set at about 8.5/10.",
 [("What senior researchers get hired for",
   "**Judgment, not method coverage.** The strongest researchers will be hired less for what methods they can run and more for **what they can stop**: saying no, slowing teams down, calling out weak logic.\n\n**Fixing inputs, not running studies.** The best will be pulled upstream to define questions, success criteria and evidence standards before any research happens — less visible work, much more valuable.\n\n**ROI becomes a must-have.** Not inherently a researcher's job, but squarely within our strengths.",
   cmp("Hired for in the past", ["Methods you can run", "Studies delivered", "Insight volume"],
       "Valued in 2026", ["What you can stop", "Questions and evidence standards set upstream", "Impact shown in cost, risk or revenue"]),
   q("What does Calabro predict senior researchers will be valued for?",
     ["What they can stop", "How many studies they run", "Which methods they have mastered", "The size of the research team they manage"],
     "Saying no, slowing teams down and calling out weak logic become core expectations.")),
  ("The split in UX maturity",
   "Companies will fall into two camps, and the gap will become impossible to ignore.\n\n**System-driven orgs** can explain decisions, trade-offs and ROI.\n**Vibes-driven orgs** keep shipping confident-looking products that underperform once they meet real market conditions.\n\nRelated: **ResearchOps will matter more than ever** — one great researcher can't fix a broken system. Teams that improve will invest in templates, decision logs, evidence standards and repeatable workflows. The ones that don't will keep burning out their strongest people.", None,
   q("What will separate the two camps of UX maturity?",
     ["Whether they can explain decisions, trade-offs and ROI", "How much they spend on research", "Whether they employ dedicated researchers", "How quickly they ship new features"],
     "One group can explain; the other ships confident-looking products that underperform.")),
  ("Four more",
   "**Synthetic users quietly fall out of favour** — what looks like insight is often automated bias confirmation. By late 2026, AI proxy users start to feel embarrassing in serious research orgs.\n\n**Heuristic reviews regain credibility as a senior skill** — expert evaluation grounded in task flow and real context, as teams tire of expensive studies answering obvious questions.\n\n**“Delight” stays misunderstood** — more bad animation, less proper labelling, more friction.\n\n**Accessibility stops being a checklist** — discussed as failure modes, legal exposure and task breakdowns instead.\n\n**Reporting gets shorter and sharper** — concise readouts, shared docs and short clips, with real recommendations.", None,
   q("Why does he expect heuristic reviews to regain credibility?",
     ["Teams tire of expensive studies answering obvious questions", "Automated tools now run them reliably", "They are cheaper than recruiting participants", "Regulators have started requiring expert review"],
     "Expert-driven evaluation grounded in task flow and real-world context."))],
 [q("Where were these predictions published?",
    ["In his 2025 year-in-review post", "As a standalone predictions article", "In a conference keynote", "In the Reframing series"],
    "The year-in-review ends with article groupings, a top five, and the predictions."),
  q("How does he score his previous year's predictions?",
    ["About 8.5 out of 10", "Roughly half right", "He doesn't revisit them", "Nine out of ten"],
    "One flat-out wrong and one partially right, by his own assessment."),
  q("What does he predict for synthetic users?",
    ["They quietly fall out of favour", "They replace early-stage interviews", "They become standard for screening", "Regulators restrict their use"],
    "“Automated bias confirmation” that starts to feel embarrassing in serious orgs."),
  q("How will accessibility be framed, in his prediction?",
    ["As failure modes, legal exposure and task breakdowns", "As a WCAG conformance target", "As a design system responsibility", "As part of the QA process"],
    "Less optional, more operational."),
  q("What does he expect to happen to research reporting?",
    ["Shorter readouts with real recommendations", "More detailed methodology sections", "A move to live dashboards", "Standardised templates across the industry"],
    "Researchers who can't summarise impact in plain language will struggle.")],
 summary="Ten predictions for 2026, from the year-in-review: judgment over methods, ROI, and a split in UX maturity.",
 title="2026 Predictions for UX")

write("behavioral-evidence",
 "When the question is usability, the best evidence comes from observing behaviour. The closer you get to real tasks, real constraints and real consequences, the more trustworthy the data.",
 [("Three categories, not two",
   "**Behavioural evidence** — task completion or failure, critical errors and where they happen, backtracking and hesitation, time on task when meaningful, moments where the user needs help, accessibility outcomes like keyboard operability.\n\n**Perception evidence** — what the participant believed the system was doing, what they thought a control meant, what goal they were pursuing, what felt missing, what they expected next.\n\n**Sentiment** — “I liked it”, “it feels modern”, “it was intuitive”, ratings and NPS-style prompts.",
   cmp("Behavioural", ["Completed or failed", "Where they backtracked", "Where they needed help"],
       "Perception and sentiment", ["What they thought a control meant", "What they expected next", "“It felt intuitive”"]),
   q("A participant says they thought the “Archive” button would delete the item. Which category is that?",
     ["Perception evidence", "Behavioural evidence", "Sentiment", "A usability defect"],
     "What they believed the system was doing — valuable, but not a record of what happened.")),
  ("The rule of thumb",
   "**If behavioural evidence and perception evidence conflict, behavioural evidence wins.**\n\nThat doesn't mean ignoring what the person said. It means interpreting the statement in light of what they did — the quote explains the behaviour, it doesn't override it.\n\nA quote from a discovery interview, a post-task rating and a screen recording of task failure are all data. Treating them as interchangeable is the mistake.", None,
   q("A user rates the task 4/5 for ease but failed it twice on video. Which do you lead with?",
     ["The observed failures", "The rating, since it's quantified", "Whichever matches other studies", "Neither — the result is inconclusive"],
     "Behavioural evidence wins; the rating then helps explain why they felt it went well.")),
  ("Why teams get this wrong",
   "Three predictable reasons, and none of them is that users lie:\n\n**1. Perception data is easy to package.** A clean quote looks good on a slide and travels well through an organisation.\n\n**2. It often aligns with what stakeholders already want to be true.** Confirmation bias turns a single quote into a decision.\n\n**3. Lean UX culture trained teams to over-index on lightweight discovery.** Interview-driven discovery crowds out observation — the reason that annoys Calabro most, because the HCI field is not new.", None,
   q("Why does perception data travel so well in organisations?",
     ["A clean quote packages easily for a slide", "It is statistically more reliable", "It is cheaper to collect", "Stakeholders request it directly"],
     "Easy to package, and it often matches what people already believe."))],
 [q("What is the rule of thumb when the two kinds of evidence conflict?",
    ["Behavioural evidence wins", "The larger sample wins", "The more recent study wins", "Treat the result as inconclusive"],
    "You still interpret the statement — you just don't let it override what happened."),
  q("Which of these is behavioural evidence?",
    ["Backtracking and hesitation", "A satisfaction rating", "What the user expected to happen", "A preference between two designs"],
    "Along with completion, errors, time on task and accessibility outcomes."),
  q("Which reason annoys Calabro most?",
    ["Lean UX over-indexing on lightweight discovery", "Stakeholders asking for quotes", "Survey tools being too easy to use", "Researchers avoiding analytics"],
    "Because the HCI field is not new — this was settled decades ago."),
  q("What makes evidence more trustworthy, according to the opening?",
    ["Real tasks, real constraints, real consequences", "A larger and more diverse sample", "Triangulating three or more methods", "Using a validated instrument"],
    "The closer you get to those, the better the data."),
  q("What is wrong with treating a quote, a rating and a screen recording as the same thing?",
    ["They answer different questions", "Quotes are less reliable than ratings", "Recordings take longer to analyse", "Ratings need a larger sample"],
    "All three are data; the error is interchangeability.")],
 summary="Behaviour, perception and sentiment are three different things — and behaviour wins when they conflict.")

write("ic",
 "His case for why senior UX researchers don't need a management title to lead.",
 [("What companies actually value",
   "A lot of companies say they value research. What they really value is **confidence**.\n\nIf you're not careful, research becomes the thing that supplies it: “go test this”, or worse, “get a quick user quote to support this deck”.\n\nThe context is the end of what he calls the **“easy money era”** of the early 2020s, when teams could build freely, fail often and try again.", None,
   q("What does Calabro say companies really value, as opposed to what they say?",
     ["Confidence", "Speed", "Evidence", "User satisfaction"],
     "Which is how research gets reduced to supplying it after the decision.")),
  ("The “customer representative” trap",
   "Under delivery pressure, PMs shortcut user data — summarising Slack messages, speaking for the customer from memory.\n\nIt isn't that they're anti-user. They're moving fast, and real research seems slow **unless someone shows them a better way**.\n\nA senior IC is positioned to do exactly that:\n\n• Sit in roadmap planning and say “before we commit, can I show you what users are actually trying to do?”\n• Reframe a feature spec into a hypothesis testable **this week** instead of next quarter\n• Take a vague business goal and show how it maps — or doesn't — to actual behaviour\n• Work **across teams**, not just within a reporting structure",
   cmp("Manager influence", ["Flows through reporting lines", "Assigns and approves", "Bounded by the org chart"],
       "Senior IC influence", ["Shows up in roadmap planning", "Reframes specs into testable hypotheses", "Works across teams"]),
   q("Why do PMs end up acting as the customer representative?",
     ["Research seems slow unless shown a better way", "They distrust research methods", "They are measured on user satisfaction", "They have been trained to do it"],
     "It's delivery pressure, not hostility to users.")),
  ("Tactics that work",
   "**Ask reframing questions** — change what the team is deciding, not just what gets tested.\n**Think outcomes over artifacts** — the deck isn't the deliverable; the decision is.\n**Get good at moving faster** — speed is what buys you the seat.\n\nHe also reframes research as a way to **de-risk investment**: not just user-centred, but cost-conscious.\n\nAnd his rule of thumb: *“if you ever find yourself talking to non-UXers about cognitive load, you're doing it wrong.”*", None,
   q("What is his rule of thumb about talking to non-UXers?",
     ["Don't talk to them about cognitive load", "Always bring a prototype", "Lead with the method", "Translate findings into personas"],
     "Speak in their terms — risk, cost, outcomes."))],
 [q("How does a senior IC reframe research for stakeholders?",
    ["As a way to de-risk investment", "As a quality gate before release", "As a service with a fixed turnaround", "As a design input"],
    "Not just user-centred, but cost-conscious."),
  q("What happens to research when a company values confidence above all?",
    ["It gets used to justify decisions already made", "It gets more budget than it needs", "It becomes the PM's responsibility", "It moves earlier in the process"],
    "“Go test this” or “get a quick user quote to support this deck”."),
  q("Which tactic is on his list?",
    ["Think outcomes over artifacts", "Publish a quarterly research report", "Run more studies per sprint", "Train PMs in research methods"],
    "Along with asking reframing questions and moving faster."),
  q("What does “work across teams” mean here?",
    ["Influence beyond your reporting structure", "Join several squads at once", "Share findings in a central repository", "Rotate between product areas"],
    "An IC's reach isn't bounded by the org chart."),
  q("What was the “easy money era”?",
    ["The early 2020s, when teams could build and fail freely", "The period before UX had job titles", "The boom in UX bootcamps", "The years before AI tooling"],
    "The context for why research is under pressure now.")],
 summary="Companies say they value research; what they value is confidence. Influence comes from reframing decisions.")

write("thankless",
 "UX work feels thankless because people adapt to improvements and forget what came before. Hedonic adaptation explains it — and makes it a sign of success rather than a problem.",
 [("What hedonic adaptation is",
   "The human tendency to **return to a stable level of satisfaction after something changes**. A positive change feels meaningful at first, then becomes the new normal.\n\nHis example: a painful five-minute daily workflow, flagged in interviews, surveys, support tickets and analytics. The team fixed it in a redesign — **time on task dropped to under two minutes** and error rates fell.\n\nThere was immediate positive feedback. Within a week, people had moved on.", None,
   q("What happened a week after the workflow was fixed?",
     ["People had moved on and treated it as normal", "Usage of the feature dropped again", "Stakeholders asked for further changes", "The old complaints returned"],
     "The new design became the assumed baseline expectation.")),
  ("Why it feels like nothing happened",
   "Once users stop thinking about the original pain points, the work that removed them **has nothing left to anchor to**.\n\nAnd product teams forget the old version almost immediately. Once the new design stabilises, the comparison point disappears for everyone.\n\nFrom the user's side it wasn't an improvement at all — it was fixing something that was broken.",
   cmp("What it feels like", ["Nobody noticed", "No credit for the work", "Was it even worth it?"],
       "What actually happened", ["The pain point stopped existing", "The comparison point disappeared", "Metrics moved and stayed moved"]),
   q("Why does the improvement stop being visible?",
     ["The comparison point disappears for everyone", "Metrics revert over time", "Users find new problems to report", "Teams stop measuring after launch"],
     "Nobody remembers the old version, so there is nothing to compare against.")),
  ("The reframe",
   "Research has shown the thankless feeling doesn't come from a weakness in the field or a lack of appreciation. **It comes from how human adaptation works.**\n\nSo the fix isn't to chase recognition. It's to adjust expectations to reality — and to note that UX impact is **easier to measure in the moment than to feel later**, which is an argument for capturing the before-and-after while the comparison still exists.", None,
   q("What does the article say about measuring impact?",
     ["It is easier to measure in the moment than to feel later", "It should be measured a quarter after launch", "Qualitative feedback measures it best", "It can't be measured reliably"],
     "Which is why you capture the comparison while it still exists."))],
 [q("What is hedonic adaptation?",
    ["Returning to a stable level of satisfaction after a change", "Preferring familiar interfaces to new ones", "Rating recent experiences more highly", "Adjusting effort to the difficulty of a task"],
    "It applies to positive and negative changes alike."),
  q("What happened to time on task in his example?",
    ["It dropped to under two minutes", "It halved", "It stayed about the same", "It rose before falling"],
    "From a painful five-minute workflow, with error rates falling too."),
  q("Where does the thankless feeling come from?",
    ["How human adaptation works", "A lack of appreciation for UX", "Weak communication by researchers", "Teams measuring the wrong things"],
    "Not a weakness in the field."),
  q("How does the user experience the improvement?",
    ["As something broken being fixed", "As a noticeable upgrade", "As a change they didn't ask for", "As a reason to use the product more"],
    "Which is why it generates no lasting credit."),
  q("What does the article suggest doing with the feeling?",
    ["Reframe expectations to match reality", "Communicate impact more loudly", "Change how teams are rewarded", "Measure satisfaction continuously"],
    "A lot of the stress around UX impact disappears with the reframe.")],
 summary="Hedonic adaptation explains why good UX gets forgotten — and why that's the work succeeding.")
